ft_strjoin

Goal

Concatenate two strings into a new allocated string.

ft_strjoin("Hello ", "World")

returns:

"Hello World"


Implementation

#include <stdlib.h>
static size_t	ft_strlen(const char *s)
{
	size_t	i;
	i = 0;
	while (s[i])
		i++;
	return (i);
}
char	*ft_strjoin(char const *s1, char const *s2)
{
	char	*res;
	size_t	i;
	size_t	j;
	if (!s1 || !s2)
		return (NULL);
	res = malloc(ft_strlen(s1) + ft_strlen(s2) + 1);
	if (!res)
		return (NULL);
	i = 0;
	while (s1[i])
	{
		res[i] = s1[i];
		i++;
	}
	j = 0;
	while (s2[j])
	{
		res[i + j] = s2[j];
		j++;
	}
	res[i + j] = '\0';
	return (res);
}

⸻

Important Concepts

You cannot modify string literals safely:

Wrong:

s1 += s2;

C strings are arrays of chars ending with '\0'.

So you allocate a new area large enough for both.

⸻

Memory Layout

s1 = "abc"
s2 = "de"
allocate:
[a][b][c][d][e][\0]

⸻

Edge Cases

Empty strings

ft_strjoin("", "")

must return allocated empty string.

⸻

NULL pointers

ft_strjoin(NULL, "abc")

should return NULL.

⸻

Tests

printf("%s\n", ft_strjoin("Hello ", "42"));
printf("%s\n", ft_strjoin("", ""));
printf("%s\n", ft_strjoin("abc", ""));

